#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151892 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbd8ae67-a4f5-5506-9254-9d2ed28b37ae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "77b7b054-6ca9-5993-b928-1526272e72c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6629" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0$." :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} + 4x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + λx^{λ} + 4x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + λ + 4] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ &= ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=c_1 \\cos(2 \\ln{x}) + c_2\\sin(2 \\ln{x})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151893 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5937a559-6911-591f-a77c-74ace6d4d659" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "af6753bc-cadb-5c39-84cd-0a56fb264b6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6630" :question/problem "Determine the general solution to the equation\n\n$t^2 y'' +5ty'+8y = 0,\\quad t\\gt 0$." :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=t^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)t^{λ - 2} + 5t \\cdot λt^{λ - 1} + 8t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)t^{λ} + 5λt^{λ} + 8t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nt^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + 5λ + 8] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + 5λ + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 4λ + 8 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 4λ + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 4λ + 4) - 4 + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 2 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -2 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t) = t^{-2} \\left(c_1 \\cos(2 \\ln{t}) + c_2 \\sin(2 \\ln{t}) \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151894 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0a76d67-24fe-577b-a395-d6374e76f269" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "829b826c-a7b1-5d7d-b68f-81d4ac4ac800" :question/math-academy-id "e-6631" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y(x)= c_1 \\cos(7 \\ln{x}) + c_2\\sin(7 \\ln{x})$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1) = -7, \\quad x > 0$." :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -7$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y^{′}|= \\frac{7}{x}(c_{2}\\cos (7\\ln x) - c_{1}\\sin (7\\ln x))$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -7$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above to get\n$-7|= 7c_{2}; c_{2}|=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = -\\sin(7 \\ln{x})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "8c74c4ab-1a4d-571b-b09c-6d9ca046a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6632" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = t(c_1 \\cos(3 \\ln{t}) + c_2\\sin(3 \\ln{t}))$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0, \\quad y'(1) = -1, \\quad t\\gt 0$." :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -1$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y^{′}|= c_{2}(\\sin (3\\ln t) + 3\\cos (3\\ln t))$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -1$ and $c_1=0$ into the above to get\n$-1|= 3c_{2}; - \\frac{1}{3}|= c_{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t) = -\\dfrac{t\\sin(3 \\ln{t}))}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbd8ae67-a4f5-5506-9254-9d2ed28b37ae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbc44ba1-4d29-54f2-8648-4859aea01f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + \\frac{c_{2}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150097} {:answer/id #uuid "ef35b9c7-b11d-5e17-9a18-c40e7ca8a8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150098} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a9796c-1095-536f-bacd-d44a252b12bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}\\cos (\\ln 2x) + c_{2}\\sin (\\ln 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150099} {:answer/id #uuid "2c51c74c-730c-572a-be68-c0c159ad8e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x^{2} + \\frac{c_{2}}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150100} {:answer/